# Rust
/target
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb
Cargo.lock.orig

# We DO commit Cargo.lock for the binary/controller crates (reproducible builds).
# Nothing to ignore there.

# Local incident bundles / test artifacts
*.ieb
# …except the frozen compatibility fixtures: their bytes are the contract
!test/fixtures/ieb/**/*.ieb
# The rest of what a bundle root or `lapilli demo` leaves behind is somebody's cluster data —
# unredacted logs, pod IPs, labels, field-manager names — so a `git add -A` in a clone must not be
# able to commit it (docs/data-handling.md).
*.summary.json
*.unsent
*.unsent.tmp
*.notified
*.ieb.owner
*.ieb.tmp
reclaimed.jsonl
reclaimed.jsonl.[0-9]
.staging-*/
# `lapilli demo` also unpacks the bundle beside the .ieb, into `<cluster-id>-<16 hex>/`, and
# `--out` defaults to the current directory (README, "The bundle lands in …").
*-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]/
/tmp
/.scratch

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Kubernetes local dev
kubeconfig
*.kubeconfig

# Go (lapilli-case). `go build ./cmd/lapilli-case` leaves the binary in the current directory.
/lapilli-case
# What `lapilli-case run` and `packets` write by default, and where a judge's verdicts tend to land.
# A run record is an agent's transcript: with `--live` it is whatever that cluster returned. The
# recorded runs that are meant to be public live under test/fixtures/case-runs/.
/results/
/packets.json
/key.json
/verdicts.json
